    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d847">

                <pb facs="1129" n="1119" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg1119"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/8–945: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hurley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-08-09T11:00:00+07:00">August 9, 1945—11
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1312. Secretary of the Treasury, Washington, from Adler.<anchor n="9"
                        xml:id="d847-f1"/> Ambassador Hurley informs me that he is strongly in favor
                    of China’s setting up a U. S. dollar half-billion currency stabilization fund,
                    provided that fund is constituted from China’s existing dollar assets. He is
                    definitely of opinion, and has told T. V. Soong, that American voice and advice
                    should be heard in management of fund. Ambassador Hurley has also discussed fund
                    with Generalissimo. [Adler.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hurley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d847-f1" n="9" xml:id="d847-f1-content">
                <p>Presumably in further answer to Department’s telegram No. 761, May 20, 1 p.m.,
                        <ref target="#pg1096">p. 1096</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
